Looking at City C's single case: news +8 gave change -7.8, suggesting an apparent negative responsiveness likely from noise; but City C is local coverage (small movements like City B, roughly 0.3-0.9 per unit news).

Using a modest positive responsiveness (~0.4) typical of local coverage, news -5 gives about -2.

{"rationale": "City C uses local coverage with small responsiveness; the single anomalous case is likely noise, so I apply a modest positive news effect giving a small decline for -5 news.", "predicted_poll": 34.0}

Let me estimate City C's responsiveness from matched pairs.

City C: local news, so smaller responsiveness. Pair 1: news ±9, changes -13.4 and -1.6. Average slope = (change diff)/(news diff) = (-13.4 - (-1.6))/(-9-9) = -11.8/-18 ≈ 0.66. Pair 2 has one point. Using slope ~0.4-0.5 (local, similar to B).

{"rationale": "Using City C's estimated local responsiveness (~0.5 per news unit) applied to net news -4 from a start of 63.8.", "predicted_poll": 61.8}

Estimating City C responsiveness from paired/available data: national coverage suggests larger movement per unit news.

Let me estimate the slope. Using pair 1: +8 gave -2.7, -8 gave -6.2; difference of 3.5 over 16 news units ≈ 0.22 per unit, but noisy. Pair 2 only one side. Averaging effect, responsiveness ~0.5-0.7 like City B.

{"rationale": "Applying City C's estimated news responsiveness (~0.5/unit) to +8 net news from a 38.5 start.", "predicted_poll": 42.0}

Let me estimate City C's responsiveness using paired cases to cancel noise.

City C is local coverage (smaller movements). Using pairs to estimate slope: Pair 1 difference in change = (-5.6)-(-7.6)=2.0 over news difference of 20, giving slope ≈0.1 per point. Applying to +7: ≈0.7 movement.

{"rationale": "City C shows weak responsiveness, so +7 news adds only a small gain to the 42.8 start.", "predicted_poll": 43.5}

Looking at City C, the two matched cases give noisy estimates, but I should infer responsiveness partly from context (local news = smaller movements, similar to City A).

City C pair: +7 news gave change -1.8, -7 news gave -0.3. Average change ≈ -1.05 (baseline drift), and the difference suggests a small slope. Local news implies small responsiveness.

{"rationale": "Local-news city implies small responsiveness, so +5 net news yields only a slight positive movement from the baseline drift.", "predicted_poll": 62.0}

Looking at City C's responsiveness: averaging pair responses gives about 2.2 change per news unit.

For -5 news: -5 × 2.2 ≈ -11, so 51.6 - 11 ≈ 40.6.

{"rationale": "City C shows high responsiveness (~2.2 per news unit), so -5 news lowers the poll by roughly 11 points from 51.6.", "predicted_poll": 40.6}

Estimating City C responsiveness per news unit from pairs, roughly averaging changes.

City C: local news, so smaller responsiveness. From pair 1 (avg magnitude): changes -3.5 and -11.1 for ±9; pair 2 +6.5 for +9. Averaging responsiveness ≈ (3.5/9 + something)... reasonable slope ~0.4-0.5.

{"rationale": "City C shows modest local-news responsiveness (~0.4/unit), so -10 news yields about -4 change from 40.6.", "predicted_poll": 36.6}

Estimating City C responsiveness from paired averages: per-pair slope ~ (avg change difference)/(news range).

Pair1: (9.6-(-6.1))/16 = 0.98; Pair2: (-1.8-(-10.2))/16 = 0.525; avg ~0.75.

{"rationale": "City C responds nationally (~0.75 per news point), so +5 news yields about +3.75 from 40.1.", "predicted_poll": 43.9}

Looking at the sign convention issue: positive news should favor the candidate, but Cities A shows negative responsiveness. Let me focus on City C directly.

City C: news +8 gave change +7.6, so responsiveness ≈ 0.95 per unit news. For news -10: change ≈ -9.5, end ≈ 50.1 - 9.5 = 40.6.

{"rationale": "Using City C's observed responsiveness (~0.95 per news unit), net news -10 yields about -9.5 change from 50.1.", "predicted_poll": 40.6}
